Z||000009||olliver||11-06-2001||05:38 PM||||i think i have to add my .02 cents here!!!!<P>First of all, you can't just build a box and slap some speaker in it and expect it to perform.  The engineers from these different manufacturers don't just spend countless number of hours designing and testing sub woofers or any speaker for that matter for their own good health.<P>Here's the best analogy i can think of.<P>A subwoofer's enclosure is like a cam in an engine.  If your cam is too large you are going to lose performance, if it's too small you are going to lose performance.  You have to know all the spec's of your engine before you just go out and slap some 'ol cam in it.<P>The same applies for a sub.  The enclosure (type, volume) for your subwoofer is DIRECTLY related to it's performance.<P>There is a reason the engineers include manufactures spec's with your sub.  With these spec's you can decide what you want your sub to be used for.  <BR>You first have to know what you want out of your sub.  Whether it be raw S.P.L. (sound pressure level) or nice S.Q. (sound quality)<BR>You can't have both.  The more spl you want the worse it's going to sound and vice versa.<P>The spec sheet will give you volume (minus the volume of the speaker) for the proper enclosure.  Designing speaker enclosures is TRULY A SCIENCE.  <P>Material of perfered choice is M.D.F (medium density fiberboard) and with only one 10" sub you are wasting money and valuable space going with anything thicker than 1/2".  In my saturn i had 2 10" QX pheonix gold in a 2 cubic foot box made of only 1/2 and it hit 126 dB's.<P>If you would like any other information regarding speaker enclosures please, please don't hesitate to ask.  I own and operate a car stereo shop up here in alberta.<P>Olliver||||reg||1
Z||000010||blazin'||11-06-2001||05:52 PM||||I'm trying to figure out what to ask, I don't know a great deal regarding car stereo systems. I would prefer to possibly have 2 10 inch JL Audio subs behind the passenger seat in my Fiero. I'd like to make the box myself, since I need a project to do in my Production Technology class I thought I'd build a sub box, afterall, It won't cost me any thing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>To be honest, I'm not really what size the box should be, since it will be in a relatively restricted area. Any suggestions? About anything? Lol....<P>At least I got a pretty good deal on my truck stereo system<P>2 Pioneer 120 watt 6x9's, 100 watt amplified 8 inch bass cannon, 45x5 watt Pioneer cd deck<BR>                 $180 cdn<p>[This message has been edited by blazin' (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||avengador1||11-06-2001||06:22 PM||||It's not size of the box you are looking for, it's volume (cubic feet, inches,etc.) This is what is required by the speaker you will be using. To determine if you need a ported box (another can of worms,formulas, and tuning) you need to know if the type speaker you will be using. if it's a free air type, no port needed, as a matter of fact you could run this type with no box, hence the name free air. Most speakers work better with some type of enclosure. Radio shack used to sell a book on designing your own speaker enclosures. It would be a good investment and get you on the right track.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Spektrum-87GT||11-06-2001||06:28 PM||||well, i had 2 twelves in two seperate boxes, one behind each seat, i spent little to no time planning the volume or anything like that. i'll tell you this... 600 watts(subs alone) is WAY too much for the fiero, it gave you a headache within minutes and it felt like someone was punching you in the back. i did build them very good though, spent a lot of man hours on it. so in conclusion, crazy bass RUINS the ride. I took them out, so much more room, so little headaches. your ears with thank you!||||reg||1
Z||000013||blazin'||11-06-2001||06:29 PM||||Thanx a lot! I'll have to make a trip to Radio Shack||||reg||1
Z||000014||olliver||11-06-2001||10:29 PM||||If I may add another .02 cents.  Sure boxes are cool behind the seats in the fiero.  But i'm sorry, i'm 6'4" 245lbs. For a box to be behind my seat i'd be steering with my knees to me ears and have no need for airbags since the steering wheel will already be implanted in my chest.  It's allright if you are 5'8" and shorter.  <P>One thing that i'm exploring is the idea of cutting away behind the passenger seat, if you peel back the carpet, you will see a welded square outline.  I've cut out the center of that square and if i remember right i think there was aprox. 2" deep to the second wall.  That is plenty of space to sit the cone of a sub in and if anyone is proficient enough or willing to experiment with fibreglass you could mold a box so that the seat doesn't have to be like 6" from the firewall.<P>For my install, i've built custom kick pannels that house 6 1/2" mid drivers and tweeters and they sit almost flush with the sidewall just infront of the door.  when i get time to carpet/vinyl them i'll snap some pictures, actually i'll snap a picture maybe tomorrow so that everyone can see what i'm talking about.<P>My best freind in toronto built an increadibly wicked box that is contoured to sit on the passenger side floor.  If you look at the center console wall on the passenger side you'll notice that it indents under the dash.  He built a box that follows the contour of the wall and runs up to the seat it sticks out like 2" from the wall.  Then there is a section that starts at the front of the seat and is angled down to the floor this section was as wide as the seat.<P>I think that his box was completely amazing.  Not only did it allow the passenger to maintain leg/foot room, but holy cow did this thing pound.  He had a boswick 10".||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bill Shenefelt||12-25-2001||11:40 AM||||Couple of problems with subwoofers in a car.  You do not need real low frequency since the wave "won't fit" in the car. A 24 cycle wave wont make it in a 25 fut room much less a car.   What most people are really after is good performance down to maybe 45 cps and then a rapid frequency cutoff to save power and speakers.  (the lowest note on a bass gutar is in the 40's.  Box size and type is very important.  Sealed boxes are usually more forgiving than ported enclosures from a volume accuracy.  For punch you need air volume movement either by the travel of the cone or the diameter of the cone.  Decent speakers include specifications including Theil Small parameters for Qt, Qms and Qes and Vas from which you can calculate ideal box volume.  A high quality(accurate sounding)bass speaker will have a Qt of 3.0 or lower.  As for box material, a big speaker needs THICK rigid walls.  For 18 inch JBL professional driver in my home I use a box of 1.5 inch thick high density particle board(8 cubic ft) braced with hardwood 2x4's.  For my car 10 inch speaker I use 3/4 inch high density cabinet grade particle board(sometimes called flake board)  A flexible enclosure wall will give you lots of box "boom" and if you can stomach Rap music(I apply the term music loosely) and just want to bother the homes you drive by then 1/2 inch will do.<BR>Box volume for a Qt of about .33 is close to equal to Vas, and at .2 it is close to 2.5 times the Vas for a ported reflex enclosure.  A Vas of under 1 cubic ft is usually a pretty small driver, 6 inches or lower.  For mid priced speakers<BR>(say $100 to $200 each), usually a manufacturer will give an ideal box size for either reflex(ported) or sealed enclosure. More than 15% different than the stated volume is going too far away and responce will suffer. A third "box" design is infinite baffle- a wall or the trunk for a car as a "box".  This gives a higher efficiency(louder per watt).  Typical of 6 x 9 car speakers.  Don't use a flimsy box unless you are happy with boomy bass.<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
