Z||000017||FieroPerformance||12-26-2001||12:42 AM||||I've got an 8" Rockford Fosgate HX2 DVC. Its RMS is 400 and peak is 800. I will be pushing it with an RF 500.2 amp that I just got for Christmas. It is in a custom built eclosure. Sealed. Approx .26 cu. FT. I will let you know how it sounds.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||88-DOHC||12-26-2001||05:28 AM||||Just adding my .02.  If you don't know what your doing, build a sealed enclosure, and make sure its completely sealed (including around the speaker hole when the speaker is mounted). Then Before mounting the speaker, buy some speaker stuffing and stuff the box. This fools the speaker into thinking its in a slightly bigger enclosure then it really is.  Sealed enclosures are by far the most forgiving. Although, it may not give you that "hard hitting" action your looking for. Particularly if the enclosure isn't the optimal size for the particular speaker that your putting it in.  I wouldn't recommend messing with the other types of enclosures unless you know what your doing. For a car as small as the Fiero, sealed enclosure is about all you really can do. Unless you go completely wild. You could try a ported enclosure, but if not done properly they will end up boomy and mushy sounding (not very good quality sounding). Isobarics are out of the question just due to simple space economics even though the benifits of Isobarics are idealy suited for small spaces. My only other suggestion would be to go with 8" subs, specificly the 8" subs made by JL Audio. If you don't mind less leg room then possibly a JL 10W6, which can be placed in as small as .4 cuft sealed enclosures.||||reg||1
